{
  "teamId": 116,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Detroit Tigers entered the season with genuine optimism, built around a wave of young talent finally arriving and the enduring presence of franchise icon Miguel Cabrera. Opening Day set the template for the entire year: thrilling swings of momentum, clutch moments from unexpected heroes, and a bullpen that repeatedly gave back what the offense scratched out. Eric Haase's go-ahead homer and Javier Báez's clutch single on April 8th captured the early excitement, but Gregory Soto's bullpen implosions would become a recurring and painful motif throughout the campaign.\n\nApril and May offered flickers of promise — Spencer Torkelson's early power, Javier Báez's three-run shot, and contributions from veterans like Jeimer Candelario kept Detroit competitive enough to stay in the conversation. But the offense, ranking among the worst in baseball with a .632 OPS, simply could not sustain pressure over a full season. The pitching staff, while posting a respectable 4.04 ERA, was undermined by a bullpen prone to catastrophic meltdowns at the worst possible moments, turning winnable games into deflating losses.\n\nThe summer months brought the arrival of Riley Greene and the occasional magic of Victor Reyes — his walk-off double in late July was the emotional high point of the season — but August was a disaster, with Detroit going a dreadful 9-18 as the roster's limitations were laid bare. The young players were learning on the job, and the growing pains were visible. The 713 runs allowed told the real story of a team whose pitching depth and defensive reliability were not yet ready to compete in a tough American League.\n\nSeptember offered a surprising reprieve, with the Tigers playing .500 ball and Harold Castro delivering a pair of clutch hits late in the month to provide some dignity. Kody Clemens' grand slam on the final day of the season was a fitting, bittersweet coda — a moment of joy in a 66-96 campaign that confirmed this team still had significant ground to cover before its young core could carry it back to relevance. The foundation is being poured, but 2022 was ultimately a year of hard lessons.",
  "chapters": [
